**Abstract class vs interface (Java):**

- **State:** an abstract class can have instance fields; an interface has only constants (`public static final`).
- **Constructors:** abstract classes have them; interfaces do not.
- **Methods:** abstract classes mix abstract and concrete methods with any visibility; interfaces have abstract, `default`, `static` and `private` methods (Java 8/9+).
- **Inheritance:** a class extends only one abstract class but can implement many interfaces.
- **Intent:** abstract class = shared base ("is-a") with common code and state; interface = a capability or contract ("can-do").

**When to use which:**
- Use an **abstract class** when related classes share state and behaviour and you want a partial implementation (template method), for example `Vehicle` with a `speed` field and a concrete `accelerate()`.
- Use an **interface** to define a contract that unrelated classes can implement, to allow multiple inheritance of type, or to let implementations be swapped (`Comparable`, `Runnable`, a `PaymentGateway`).
- Prefer interfaces for APIs and add an abstract class as an optional skeletal implementation when helpful.

```java
interface Payment { void pay(double amount); }
abstract class Vehicle {
    protected int speed;
    void accelerate() { speed += 10; }
    abstract void honk();
}
class Car extends Vehicle implements Payment {
    void honk() { System.out.println("Beep"); }
    public void pay(double a) { System.out.println("Paid " + a); }
}
```

**Runtime polymorphism (dynamic method dispatch).** A call on a superclass or interface reference executes the **overriding method of the actual object** at run time.

```java
Vehicle v = new Car();
v.honk();   // runs Car.honk(), decided at run time by the object's real type
```

It differs from compile-time polymorphism (method overloading), which is resolved by the compiler from argument types. Fields and `static` or `private` methods are not dispatched dynamically. It enables code that depends on abstractions and can be extended without modification.
